sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch10-21> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Despite the complicated construction of the brain, there is a core to human thinking that is largely independent of neural details and is based on rather simple rules, with nothing fundamentally special about the processes involved.";
    dc:type "model-of-nature";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The core of human thinking is based on rather simple rules";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 10, “Processes of Perception and Analysis”; section “Human Thinking” (from p. 620), p. 628. Scope: human thinking and its emulation by artificial systems"
}
